.TH mmedit 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
mmedit \- the MMBasic full-screen editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mmedit
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mmedit
is MMBasic's own editor, working the way it does on a PicoMite: a
full-screen editor with the function keys along the bottom, for people
who already know it.
.PP
It is meant for BASIC but will edit any text file.
.SH THE F2 KEY
On a PicoMite
.B F2
is "save, exit and run", and the interpreter took the program straight
back.  Here it is
.BR "cc \-r" :
.B mmedit
saves, leaves the editor and hands the file to the compiler, which runs
the program if it built.  A BASIC file goes through
.BR mmbc (1)
on the way, so one keystroke is the whole build:
.sp
.nf
     # mmedit prog.bas          F2
     cc -r prog.bas
     wrote prog.mb.c
     ..........................
     hello from prog
.fi
.sp
A compile error stops it there, with the errors on the screen and the
editor gone - unlike the interpreter, which put you back at the offending
line.
.PP
Only the four source extensions are offered to the compiler: .bas,
\&.BAS, .c and .C.  With any other name
.B mmedit
says there is nothing to compile and exits.  Ctrl-W does the same as
.BR F2 ,
as it does in MMBasic.
.SH NOTES
The function keys are the ones MMBasic uses.  A terminal that sends a
different escape sequence for F1 to F4 than the console does is the
usual reason a key seems dead;
.B mmedit
accepts both of the common spellings.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR vi (1),
.BR ed (1),
.BR cc (1),
.BR mmbc (1)
